<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Turkic Khaganate</h1><p class="st-meta">603 CE – 742 CE (approx.) · capital Suyab</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western half of the Turkic empire, which ruled the steppe from the Altai to the Volga and the oasis cities of Sogdiana and the Tarim.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a>, founded in 552 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a>, gave its western lands to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istami">Istämi</a>, who with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire</a>. Rivalries within the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a> led to a formal split around 603, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tardu-shad">Tardu</a>'s successors ruled the west independently of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> ruled from summer pastures and courts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a> in the Chu valley over the 'On Oq', ten arrows or tribes in two wings. Sogdian merchants and scribes served them, and they sent embassies to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 627. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tong-yabghu-khagan">Tong Yabghu</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> met near Suyab around 628 to 630, the khaganate reached its height, collecting tribute from Sogdian cities and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tong-yabghu-khagan">Tong Yabghu</a>'s murder, civil wars divided the tribes, and in 657 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-dingfang">Su Dingfang</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-helu">Ashina Helu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina</a> were installed as Tang puppets until about 742; power passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turgesh-khaganate">Turgesh Khaganate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluk Yabgu</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 603 CE – 742 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:71.70%;width:2.77%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>603 CE – 742 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Suyab</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Istämi, Tardu, Tong Yabghu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Turkic, Sogdian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Onoq, Western Turks, On Oq</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=672&amp;focus=western-turkic-khaganate" aria-label="Western Turkic Khaganate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="65